NNAAMMEE
     cu - call up and converse with another system over a serial
     line

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ccuu [-hh] [-ll _l_i_n_e] [-ss _s_p_e_e_d] [_p_r_o_f_i_l_e]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ccuu turns your terminal into a terminal on a serial line, so
     that you can converse with a modem or with whatever system
     answers on the other end.  By default it opens /ddeevv/mmooddeemm at
     1200 baud in full-duplex mode and connects your terminal to
     it.

     If a _p_r_o_f_i_l_e is named on the command line, ccuu reads `~' com-
     mands from that file (see PPrrooffiillee CCoommmmaannddss below) before
     opening the port, typically to set the line, speed, and
     duplex before dialing.

     -hh   Run in half-duplex mode: echo locally every character
          sent to the remote system.  Use this if the remote sys-
          tem does not echo what you type.

     -ll _l_i_n_e
          Use _l_i_n_e instead of the default /ddeevv/mmooddeemm.

     -ss _s_p_e_e_d
          Set the serial port to _s_p_e_e_d baud (one of 50, 75, 110,
          134, 150, 200, 300, 600, 1200, 1800, 2000, 2400, 3600,
          4800, 7200, 9600, or 19200).  The default is 1200.

ccuu CCoommmmaannddss
     While connected, a line beginning with a tilde (~) is read
     by ccuu itself instead of being sent to the remote system:

     ~#   A comment; the rest of the line is ignored.

     ~~   Send a single literal tilde to the remote system.

     ~!   Start a shell on the local system.

     ~<_f_i_l_e
          Redirect further local input from _f_i_l_e instead of the
          terminal.

     ~>_f_i_l_e
          Redirect what the remote system sends into _f_i_l_e. Write
          >> _f_i_l_e to append instead of overwrite, or >: _f_i_l_e to
          redirect silently, without also echoing to the screen.

     ~>   End output redirection begun with ~>.

     ~mm   Search the incoming stream for a pattern.  Not im-
          plemented in this version; it prints ``Pattern matching
          not supported''.


